[interviewer · ANALYSIS · 0:18] Okay. You asked about this earlier: COGS is 58% of revenue today, up from 42% two years ago. Labor is 22% of revenue, and it’s been stable. Overhead is 14% of revenue, up slightly from 12%. Given those figures, which line is driving the decline, and what would you want to understand next?

[candidate · 0:23] COGS is clearly the driver. Let me check it adds up: two years ago 42 + 22 + 12 = 76% of revenue in costs, leaving 24% — that matches the old margin. Today 58 + 22 + 14 = 94%, leaving 6%. So of the 18-point decline, 16 points come from COGS and 2 from overhead. COGS is basically the entire story.

Next I'd want to break COGS open. For a coffee chain I'd expect: green coffee beans, dairy, food items, and packaging/cups. I'd want each as a percent of revenue across both years to see which component moved.

Two hypotheses I'd test. One, input prices rose — coffee and dairy are volatile commodities, so is this a market-wide spike and are they hedged or buying spot? Two, mix shifted — maybe they pushed more food or higher-cost specialty drinks with worse margins.

And the other side: have menu prices moved at all in two years? If input costs rose and they held prices flat, that alone explains it.

